To get a cyberpunk color scheme border in Niri (https://www.google.com/search?kgmid=%2Fg%2F11yjl8x6gh&q=Niri), use high-contrast neon pairings like cyan to magenta or vivid purple to electric blue.
Update your config.kdl using one of the stylized combinations below.

Option 1: The Classic Neon Outrun (Cyan to Magenta)

This is the ultimate cyberpunk look, utilizing a 45-degree angle to sweep across the window edges.
kdl
layout {
    border {
        width 4

        // Neon Cyan to Vivid Magenta/Pink
        active-gradient from="#00f3ff" to="#ff00a0" angle=45

        // Dark, muted tone for inactive windows to make the active window pop
        inactive-gradient from="#1a1c23" to="#251b2e" angle=45
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Option 2: Deep Netrunner (Purple to Electric Blue)

A slightly colder, matrix-adjacent grid runner aesthetic.
kdl
layout {
    border {
        width 4

        // Deep Violet to Electric Sky Blue
        active-gradient from="#8a2be2" to="#00dfff" angle=45

        // Muted gray-blue for inactive windows
        inactive-gradient from="#20222c" to="#182730" angle=45
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Option 3: Tox-Green & Amber (Edgerunner Style)

If you prefer the gritty, industrial cyberpunk look, try pairing a toxic neon green with a bright hazard amber.
kdl
layout {
    border {
        width 4

        // Toxic Neon Green to Hazard Orange/Amber
        active-gradient from="#39ff14" to="#ffaa00" angle=45

        inactive-gradient from="#1b241c" to="#26221a" angle=45
    }
}
Use code with caution.

💡 Dynamic Workspace Gradient Trick

If you want your windows to look like they are moving through a massive neon grid, change the relative-to property to "workspace-view". This locks the gradient to your monitor's viewport so individual windows shift colors dynamically as you scroll or resize them:
kdl
active-gradient from="#00f3ff" to="#ff00a0" angle=45 relative-to="workspace-view"
Use code with caution.
